## 1. Project Layout

A template project is a Rust workspace with three moving parts:

```
your-project/
├── Cargo.toml            # workspace + [patch.crates-io] if needed
├── tally/                # (optional) pure algorithm crate, no template ABI
│   ├── Cargo.toml
│   └── src/lib.rs
├── templates/
│   └── your_template/
│       ├── Cargo.toml    # crate-type = ["cdylib"], release profile
│       ├── src/lib.rs    # #[template] module
│       └── tests/test.rs # in-process tooling tests
└── client/
    └── integration/      # (optional) testnet client, CI-only
        ├── Cargo.toml
        └── src/main.rs
```

### The template crate's Cargo.toml

```toml
[package]
name = "your_template"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[dependencies]
tari_template_lib = "0.31"          # use the current minor version from crates.io
serde = "1"                         # only needed for structs outside the #[template] module

[dev-dependencies]
tari_template_test_tooling = "0.39" # use the current minor version from crates.io
# Do NOT add tari_ootle_transaction separately: test tooling re-exports it
# as tari_template_test_tooling::transaction

[lib]
crate-type = ["cdylib"]             # cdylib ONLY — see section 2

[profile.release]
opt-level = 's'     # Optimize for size.
lto = true          # Enable Link Time Optimization.
codegen-units = 1   # Reduce number of codegen units to increase optimizations.
panic = 'abort'     # Abort on panic.
strip = true        # Strip symbols and debug info.
```

> **Struct placement gotcha:** the `#[template]` macro treats the first struct in the template module as the component. Ancillary structs defined *inside* the template module must come **after** the component struct, or live in their own module and be `use`d. Ancillary structs defined *outside* the template module need `#[derive(serde::Serialize, serde::Deserialize)]` and `serde` as a dependency.

