## 7. Testing Templates

### In-process testing is the primary mechanism

`tari_template_test_tooling` compiles your template to WASM and runs it against the real execution engine in-process. Kelketek's guidance: integration tests against testnet are "overkill for routine testing" — keep them, but label them CI-only; the standard test library handles everything in an orderly way.

```rust
use tari_template_test_tooling::TemplateTest;
use tari_template_test_tooling::transaction::args;

#[test]
fn cast_and_tally() {
    let mut test = TemplateTest::new(".", ["."]);
    let (account, owner_proof, secret_key) = test.create_funded_account();
    let template_addr = test.get_template_address("RankedVote");

    let transaction = test.transaction()
        .call_function(template_addr, "new", args![...])
        .put_last_instruction_output_on_workspace("component")
        .call_method("component", "cast_ballot", args![...])
        .build_and_seal(&secret_key);

    let result = test.execute_expect_success(transaction, vec![owner_proof]);
}
```

### Adversarial tests are mandatory

Kelketek's list (round 1): wrong token type, double-cast, cast after close, nonsense values (no candidates, no voters). The RCV template ended with: zero voter count, zero candidates, more winners than candidates, ballot after close, ballot after expiration, end-expired before deadline, wrong token while active, invalid rankings (out-of-range id, duplicated candidate, wrong length).

```rust
let tx = test.transaction()
    .call_method(component_addr, "cast_ballot", args![wrong_bucket, ranking])
    .build_and_seal(&secret_key);

let result = test.execute_expect_failure(tx, vec![proof]);
assert!(result.reject_reason.contains("Wrong resource type"));
```

### Time control

Epoch-based rules (expiration) are testable with `set_virtual_substate`:

```rust
// advance the virtual epoch past expires_at_epoch
test.set_virtual_substate(...); // see test tooling docs for the epoch accessor
let tx = ...; // cast_ballot
test.execute_expect_failure(tx, vec![proof]);
```

### The rollback property

Failed transactions roll back completely — inputs (including stealth UTXOs) are returned. Adversarial tests can reuse the same UTXO across multiple failure attempts, which makes stealth-flavored adversarial tests cheap.

### Known test-tooling limitations

- The tooling hardcodes `minimum_value_promise = 0` for mint helpers; promise-aware mint tests must call `create_transfer_statement` directly.
- The tooling's transaction builder (`builder_localnet`) passes `Epoch(100)` as `max_epoch` — fine for tests, but remember clients must do it themselves.

